        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/.rp1/work/features/add a simple yaml validator command without adding new deps/requirements.md",
        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator CLI Command\n\n**Feature ID**: add a simple yaml validator command without adding new deps\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-02-20\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nProvide developers with a simple CLI command to validate YAML files for syntactic correctness during local development. The command accepts a file path, checks whether the YAML content is well-formed and parseable, and reports success or failure with actionable error details. The solution must not introduce any new production dependencies, relying only on capabilities already available in the Bun runtime and the JavaScript/TypeScript standard ecosystem.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration or data files currently have no built-in way within this project to quickly verify that a YAML file is syntactically correct. Malformed YAML can cause silent failures, confusing error messages downstream, or broken pipelines. A lightweight, zero-dependency validation command integrated into the project would catch these issues early in the development workflow.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- **Reduced debugging time**: Developers catch YAML syntax errors immediately at the point of authoring, rather than discovering them later during execution or deployment.\n- **Lower friction**: No need to install external tools or leave the project's toolchain to validate YAML.\n- **Minimal maintenance burden**: Zero new production dependencies means no additional supply chain risk, no version conflicts, and no increase in install size.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Time to validate a single YAML file | Under 1 second for files up to 1 MB |\n| Developer adoption within project | Used as part of regular development workflow |\n| False positives (valid YAML reported as invalid) | 0% |\n| False negatives (invalid YAML reported as valid) | 0% for basic YAML syntax errors |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Interaction |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------|\n| Developer | Any developer working on or with this project who authors or edits YAML files | Runs the validate command from the terminal during development |\n| CI/CD Pipeline (future) | Automated systems that may invoke the command as a build step | Consumes exit codes to gate pipeline progression (not in MVP scope but enabled by exit code support) |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Developers | Fast, reliable syntax checking with clear error messages |\n| Project Maintainers | Minimal maintenance overhead; no new production dependencies to track or update |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A CLI command that accepts a single YAML file path as an argument\n- Syntax-level validation (well-formed, parseable YAML)\n- Clear success message when YAML is valid\n- Clear error message with line number information when YAML is invalid\n- Process exit code: 0 for valid, 1 for invalid\n- Appropriate error handling for missing files, unreadable files, and empty input\n- Integration with the existing project script/command structure\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (checking required keys, value types, structure conformance)\n- Batch validation of multiple files or glob pattern support\n- Stdin input or raw string input\n- YAML formatting, linting, or auto-fixing\n- Advanced YAML features beyond what the runtime natively supports (e.g., custom tags)\n- GUI or web-based interface\n- Watch mode or file system monitoring\n- YAML-to-JSON conversion or any transformation output\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The Bun runtime environment provides or can provide sufficient YAML parsing capability without adding production dependencies\n- Developers have terminal access and are comfortable running CLI commands\n- YAML files to be validated are stored on the local filesystem\n- Basic YAML syntax coverage (scalars, mappings, sequences, comments, multi-line strings) is sufficient for developer needs\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-001: File Path Acceptance\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must accept a single file path as a positional CLI argument identifying the YAML file to validate.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need a straightforward way to point the tool at a specific file without complex flag syntax.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Running the command with a valid file path argument targets that file for validation.\n  - AC-2: Running the command with no file path argument produces a usage message explaining expected input.\n\n### FR-002: YAML Syntax Validation\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must parse the contents of the specified file and determine whether the content is syntactically valid YAML.\n- **Rationale**: The core purpose of the feature is to confirm YAML is well-formed before it is consumed by other tools or processes.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: A file containing valid YAML syntax is reported as valid.\n  - AC-2: A file containing invalid YAML syntax (e.g., bad indentation, unclosed quotes, invalid characters) is reported as invalid.\n  - AC-3: An empty file is handled gracefully (empty content is valid YAML per the spec).\n\n### FR-003: Success Output\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When the YAML file is syntactically valid, the command must print a clear success confirmation message to stdout.\n- **Rationale**: Developers need positive confirmation that their file passed validation, not just the absence of errors.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: A success message is printed that includes the file path/name.\n  - AC-2: The message is concise and unambiguous (e.g., indicates the file is valid YAML).\n\n### FR-004: Error Output with Line Information\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: When the YAML file contains syntax errors, the command must print an error message to stderr that includes the nature of the error and the line number where the error was detected.\n- **Rationale**: Line number information allows developers to navigate directly to the problem location, reducing debugging time significantly.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: The error message includes the file path/name.\n  - AC-2: The error message includes the line number of the detected error (where available from the parser).\n  - AC-3: The error message includes a human-readable description of the syntax problem.\n\n### FR-005: Process Exit Codes\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer, CI/CD Pipeline\n- **Requirement**: The command must exit with code 0 when the YAML is valid and exit with code 1 when the YAML is invalid or an error occurs.\n- **Rationale**: Standard exit codes enable integration with shell scripts, CI/CD pipelines, and other automated tooling that checks command success/failure.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: Exit code is 0 after successful validation of a valid YAML file.\n  - AC-2: Exit code is 1 after detecting invalid YAML syntax.\n  - AC-3: Exit code is 1 when the specified file does not exist or cannot be read.\n\n### FR-006: File Error Handling\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Developer\n- **Requirement**: The command must handle file-level errors gracefully, including: file not found, file not readable (permissions), and path pointing to a directory instead of a file.\n- **Rationale**: Developers may mistype paths or point to wrong locations; clear error messages prevent confusion with YAML syntax errors.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: A \"file not found\" scenario produces a clear error message indicating the file does not exist.\n  - AC-2: A permissions error produces a message indicating the file cannot be read.\n  - AC-3: File-level errors are distinguishable from YAML syntax errors in the output.\n\n### FR-007: No New Production Dependencies\n- **Priority**: Must Have\n- **User Type**: Project Maintainer\n- **Requirement**: The feature must be implemented without adding any new entries to the `dependencies` field of `package.json`. New `devDependencies` (such as type definitions) are permitted.\n- **Rationale**: The project prioritizes a minimal dependency footprint to reduce supply chain risk, maintenance burden, and install size.\n- **Acceptance Criteria**:\n  - AC-1: The `dependencies` field in `package.json` has no new entries after the feature is implemented.\n  - AC-2: The YAML parsing capability comes from the runtime or from code authored within the project.\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Target |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Validation of a typical YAML file (under 100 KB) | Completes in under 500 milliseconds |\n| Validation of a large YAML file (up to 1 MB) | Completes in under 1 second |\n| Startup overhead | Negligible beyond normal Bun script startup time |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n- The command must only read files; it must never write to or modify any file.\n- The command must not execute or eval any content found within YAML files.\n- File access must be limited to the explicitly provided path argument.\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n- The command invocation syntax must be intuitive and follow common CLI conventions.\n- Error messages must be written in plain English and be actionable.\n- A usage/help hint must appear when the command is invoked incorrectly (e.g., no arguments).\n- The command must be runnable through the project's existing script infrastructure (e.g., `bun run`).\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n- No specific regulatory compliance requirements apply to this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate a Correct YAML File\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** run a validation command against a YAML file I have written\n- **So that** I can confirm it is syntactically correct before using it in my project\n\n**Acceptance Scenario**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with correct syntax exists at a known path\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate command with that file path\n- THEN the command prints a success message confirming the file is valid\n- AND the process exits with code 0\n\n### STORY-002: Identify Syntax Errors in a YAML File\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** see where a syntax error is located when my YAML file is malformed\n- **So that** I can quickly navigate to the problem and fix it\n\n**Acceptance Scenario**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error (e.g., bad indentation on line 15) exists at a known path\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate command with that file path\n- THEN the command prints an error message indicating the file is invalid\n- AND the error message includes the line number where the error was detected\n- AND the error message includes a description of the syntax problem\n- AND the process exits with code 1\n\n### STORY-003: Handle Missing File Gracefully\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** receive a clear error when I provide a wrong file path\n- **So that** I know the issue is with the path, not with YAML syntax\n\n**Acceptance Scenario**:\n- GIVEN no file exists at the specified path\n- WHEN the developer runs the validate command with that nonexistent path\n- THEN the command prints an error message stating the file was not found\n- AND the process exits with code 1\n\n### STORY-004: Get Usage Help When No Arguments Provided\n- **As a** developer\n- **I want to** see usage instructions when I run the command without arguments\n- **So that** I know how to use the command correctly\n\n**Acceptance Scenario**:\n- GIVEN the developer runs the validate command with no arguments\n- WHEN the command starts\n- THEN it prints a usage message showing the expected syntax (e.g., the expected file path argument)\n- AND the process exits with code 1\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule | Rationale |\n|---------|------|-----------|\n| BR-001 | The command must never modify the file being validated | Validation is a read-only operation; accidental modification would break developer trust |\n| BR-002 | Empty YAML files are considered valid | Per the YAML specification, an empty document is valid |\n| BR-003 | Validation is syntax-only; no semantic or schema checks | Keeps scope minimal and avoids the need for schema definitions |\n| BR-004 | One file per invocation | Simplifies the interface and keeps the MVP focused |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Dependency/Constraint | Type | Impact |\n|-----------------------|------|--------|\n| Bun runtime | Runtime dependency | The project runs on Bun; YAML parsing must work within Bun's available APIs and JavaScript built-ins |\n| No new production dependencies | Hard constraint | YAML parsing must be achieved without adding packages to `dependencies` |\n| Existing project structure | Soft constraint | The command should integrate naturally with the existing `src/` directory layout and `package.json` scripts |\n| YAML parsing capability within Bun/JS | Assumption | It is assumed that sufficient YAML parsing capability exists or can be authored without external libraries |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Who is the primary user? | Developers using the CLI during development | User clarification |\n| What level of validation? | Syntax only -- check if YAML is well-formed and parseable | User clarification |\n| What is the input source? | File path as a CLI argument (e.g., `validate <file.yaml>`) | User clarification |\n| Does \"no new deps\" include devDependencies? | No -- devDependencies like type definitions are permitted; only production dependencies are restricted | User clarification |\n| Should the command return exit codes? | Yes, 0 for valid and 1 for invalid (standard CLI practice) | User clarification (recommended default accepted) |\n| Is batch validation needed? | No, single file is sufficient for MVP | User clarification (recommended default accepted) |\n| What should the output look like? | Success/error message with line numbers on failure | User clarification (recommended default accepted) |\n| What YAML features must be supported? | Basic YAML support sufficient -- whatever Bun/JS built-ins can handle | User clarification (recommended default accepted) |\n"
