        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator CLI Command\n\n**Feature ID**: add a simple yaml validator command without adding new deps\n**Created**: 2026-02-20\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing strategy | Custom TypeScript parser written within the project | FR-007 mandates zero new production dependencies. Bun and JavaScript have no built-in YAML parser. User explicitly confirmed this approach over adding js-yaml. | js-yaml npm package (mature, full spec coverage, but violates FR-007); yaml npm package (same issue); shelling out to system tool (fragile, not portable) |\n| D2 | Parser internal architecture | Line-by-line state machine with indentation stack | Simpler to implement than a full recursive descent parser or tokenizer. Sufficient for basic YAML syntax validation. Natural fit for reporting line numbers on errors. Estimated 200-400 lines. | Full recursive descent parser (higher complexity, overkill for syntax-only validation); regex-based line matching (too fragile for nested structures and multi-line scalars); character-by-character tokenizer (more complex, better for full parsing but unnecessary here) |\n| D3 | Source file structure | Flat in `src/` directory: `src/yaml-parser.ts`, `src/validate-yaml.ts` | Existing project uses flat `src/` structure with `index.ts` and `utils.ts`. No precedent for subdirectories. Minimal file count keeps things simple. | `src/commands/validate-yaml.ts` with `src/lib/yaml-parser.ts` (introduces directory structure not present in codebase); single file combining parser and CLI (violates separation of concerns, harder to test) |\n| D4 | Parser public API | Single `validateYaml(content: string): YamlValidationResult` function returning a result object | Pure function with no side effects. Easy to unit test. Clear contract via TypeScript interface. Caller decides what to do with result (print, log, etc.). | Throwing exceptions on invalid YAML (less explicit control flow, harder to distinguish error types in tests); event/callback-based API (unnecessary complexity for synchronous parsing); class-based parser with methods (over-engineered for a single operation) |\n| D5 | CLI command invocation | `bun run validate` via package.json `scripts.validate` entry | Follows the existing project pattern where all commands are package.json scripts (dev, test, lint, format). Discoverable via `npm/bun run` listing. Standard developer workflow. | Standalone binary / bin entry (overkill for project-internal tool); alias in shell config (not portable, not project-scoped); `bun run src/validate-yaml.ts` directly (works but less discoverable and less consistent with existing patterns) |\n| D6 | File reading API | `Bun.file()` with `.exists()` and `.text()` | Bun-native file API. Already available in the runtime. Clean and ergonomic. Supports async operations if needed. | Node.js `fs.readFileSync` (works in Bun but not idiomatic); `fs.promises.readFile` (works but Bun.file is preferred in Bun projects); `Bun.read` (not the standard API name) |\n| D7 | Testing framework and approach | `bun:test` with describe/test/expect; co-located test files | Existing project uses exactly this pattern (`utils.test.ts` alongside `utils.ts`). No additional dependencies needed. | Jest (would require additional devDependency); Vitest (same issue); manual test scripts (less structured, no assertions) |\n| D8 | Output stream routing | Success messages to stdout; error/usage messages to stderr | Standard UNIX CLI convention. Enables piping stdout to other tools while errors remain visible. Enables scripting via exit codes without parsing output. | Everything to stdout (breaks convention, makes scripting harder); structured JSON output (over-engineered for a simple CLI tool; could be added later if needed) |\n| D9 | YAML spec coverage scope | Basic YAML subset: mappings, sequences, scalars, quotes, comments, multi-line scalars, flow collections, document markers | Requirements explicitly scope to \"basic YAML syntax coverage\" and \"whatever Bun/JS built-ins can handle.\" Covering the full YAML 1.2 spec in a custom parser would be disproportionate effort with diminishing returns. | Full YAML 1.2 spec (extremely complex, hundreds of edge cases, not justified for this use case); minimal check only (too many false negatives) |\n| D10 | Empty file handling | Treated as valid YAML (exit code 0) | Per YAML specification, an empty document is valid YAML. Explicitly stated in BR-002 of the requirements. | Treating empty as invalid (violates YAML spec); treating empty as a warning (adds complexity, not requested) |\n\n## Interactive Decision: User-Confirmed\n\n| Decision | Options Presented | User Choice | Session Context |\n|----------|-------------------|-------------|-----------------|\n| D1 - YAML parsing strategy | A) Custom TypeScript parser (zero deps) vs B) Add js-yaml package | Option A - Custom parser | AFK_MODE=false; user was asked and explicitly confirmed Option A |\n"
