        "content": "# Feature Verification Report #1\n\n**Generated**: 2026-02-21T01:13:48Z\n**Feature ID**: add a simple yaml validator command without adding new deps\n**Verification Scope**: all\n**KB Context**: Warning - Not loaded (no `.rp1/context/` directory exists; consider running `/knowledge-build`)\n**Field Notes**: Warning - Not available (no `field-notes.md` in feature directory)\n\n## Executive Summary\n- Overall Status: PARTIAL\n- Acceptance Criteria: 16/18 verified (89%)\n- Implementation Quality: HIGH\n- Ready for Merge: NO (documentation tasks TD1 and TD2 are incomplete)\n\n## Field Notes Context\n**Field Notes Available**: No\n\n### Documented Deviations\nThe `tasks.md` file documents one intentional deviation for T2: \"Used node:fs/promises stat instead of Bun.file().exists() to properly distinguish directories from missing files (exists() returns false for directories, which conflates the two error cases).\" This is a sound engineering decision recorded in task implementation summaries, though no formal `field-notes.md` was created.\n\n### Undocumented Deviations\nNone found. The implementation closely follows the design document. The `stat` deviation noted above is documented in tasks.md.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria Verification\n\n### FR-001: File Path Acceptance\n\n**AC (FR-001-1)**: Running the command with a valid file path argument targets that file for validation.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:14 - `process.argv[2]`\n- Evidence: The CLI reads `process.argv[2]` as the file path, resolves it via `resolve(filePath)` at line 21, then passes it through the full validation pipeline. Live test confirmed: `bun run validate /tmp/valid_test.yaml` outputs `Valid YAML: /tmp/valid_test.yaml` with exit code 0.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-001-2)**: Running the command with no file path argument produces a usage message explaining expected input.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:16-19\n- Evidence: When `filePath` is falsy, the CLI prints `Usage: bun run validate <file.yaml>` to stderr and exits with code 1. Live test confirmed: running `bun run validate` with no arguments outputs the usage message to stderr.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n### FR-002: YAML Syntax Validation\n\n**AC (FR-002-1)**: A file containing valid YAML syntax is reported as valid.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/yaml-parser.ts`:281-458 - `validateYaml()` function\n- Evidence: The `validateYaml` function implements a line-by-line state machine that validates YAML syntax. 34 unit test cases in `yaml-parser.test.ts` cover valid YAML scenarios (simple mappings, nested mappings, sequences, comments, quoted strings, block scalars, flow collections, document markers, boolean/null values, anchors/aliases, directives). All 34 valid-YAML tests pass. CLI integration test with a valid file also passes.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-002-2)**: A file containing invalid YAML syntax (e.g., bad indentation, unclosed quotes, invalid characters) is reported as invalid.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/yaml-parser.ts`:303-315 (tab detection), 348-355 (quote check), 357-365 (flow collection check), 382-404 (indentation validation)\n- Evidence: The parser detects: tab indentation (lines 303-315), unterminated quoted strings via `checkQuoteBalance` (lines 99-161), unclosed/unexpected flow collections via `updateFlowDepth` (lines 204-265), and invalid indentation (lines 382-404). 11 invalid-YAML test cases pass, covering all specified error types. CLI integration test with invalid YAML also passes.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-002-3)**: An empty file is handled gracefully (empty content is valid YAML per the spec).\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/yaml-parser.ts`:282-284\n- Evidence: The function returns `{ valid: true }` immediately when `content.length === 0`. Unit test \"empty string\" at `yaml-parser.test.ts`:78-81 confirms this. CLI integration test \"empty YAML file prints success and exits with code 0\" also confirms. Live test: `bun run validate /tmp/empty_test.yaml` outputs `Valid YAML:` with exit code 0.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n### FR-003: Success Output\n\n**AC (FR-003-1)**: A success message is printed that includes the file path/name.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:45\n- Evidence: Line 45: `console.log(\\`Valid YAML: ${resolvedPath}\\`)` prints the resolved file path in the success message. CLI integration tests at `validate-yaml.test.ts`:57-64 and :101-105 verify the output contains both \"Valid YAML:\" and the file path. Live test confirmed.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-003-2)**: The message is concise and unambiguous (e.g., indicates the file is valid YAML).\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:45\n- Evidence: The output format is `Valid YAML: {filePath}` which is concise, unambiguous, and clearly indicates the file is valid YAML. Matches the format specified in the design document.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n### FR-004: Error Output with Line Information\n\n**AC (FR-004-1)**: The error message includes the file path/name.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:55\n- Evidence: Line 55: `console.error(\\`Invalid YAML: ${resolvedPath}\\`)` includes the resolved file path. CLI integration test at `validate-yaml.test.ts`:107-111 verifies the stderr output contains the file path. Live test confirmed.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-004-2)**: The error message includes the line number of the detected error.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:50-56\n- Evidence: Lines 50-53 construct the location string as `line ${error.line}` (or `line ${error.line}, column ${error.column}` when column is available). Line 56 prints `Error on ${location}: ${error.message}`. CLI integration test at `validate-yaml.test.ts`:71 asserts the stderr matches `/line \\d+/`. Live test confirmed: `Error on line 2, column 1: Tab characters are not allowed for indentation in YAML`.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-004-3)**: The error message includes a human-readable description of the syntax problem.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:56, `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/yaml-parser.ts` (various error messages)\n- Evidence: The error message from the parser is included in the output. Error messages are human-readable: \"Tab characters are not allowed for indentation in YAML\", \"Unterminated quoted string\", \"Unclosed flow collection\", \"Unexpected closing bracket\", \"Invalid indentation: expected N spaces, found M\". All are descriptive and actionable.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n### FR-005: Process Exit Codes\n\n**AC (FR-005-1)**: Exit code is 0 after successful validation of a valid YAML file.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:46\n- Evidence: Line 46: `process.exit(0)` is called when `result.valid` is true. CLI integration test at `validate-yaml.test.ts`:60 asserts `exitCode` is 0. Live test confirmed exit code 0.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-005-2)**: Exit code is 1 after detecting invalid YAML syntax.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:57\n- Evidence: Line 57: `process.exit(1)` is called when YAML is invalid. CLI integration test at `validate-yaml.test.ts`:69 asserts `exitCode` is 1. Live test confirmed exit code 1 for invalid YAML.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-005-3)**: Exit code is 1 when the specified file does not exist or cannot be read.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:31,39\n- Evidence: Line 31: `process.exit(1)` on file not found. Line 39: `process.exit(1)` on read failure. CLI integration tests verify exit code 1 for nonexistent file (line 79), directory path (line 96). Live tests confirmed.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n### FR-006: File Error Handling\n\n**AC (FR-006-1)**: A \"file not found\" scenario produces a clear error message indicating the file does not exist.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:29-32\n- Evidence: When `stat()` throws (file does not exist), the catch block at line 29 prints `Error: File not found - {resolvedPath}` to stderr. CLI integration test at `validate-yaml.test.ts`:80 asserts stderr contains \"File not found\". Live test confirmed: `Error: File not found - /tmp/nonexistent_file.yaml`.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-006-2)**: A permissions error produces a message indicating the file cannot be read.\n- Status: PARTIAL\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:37-40\n- Evidence: Line 37-40 wraps `Bun.file(resolvedPath).text()` in a try/catch that prints `Error: Cannot read file - {resolvedPath}`. This would handle permission errors during the read phase. However, if `stat()` itself fails due to permissions (EACCES), the catch block at line 29-32 would print \"File not found\" rather than \"Cannot read file\", which could be misleading for a permissions error at the stat level. No test exists for the permissions error scenario specifically.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: A permissions error on `stat()` would produce a \"File not found\" message instead of \"Cannot read file\". This is a minor edge case -- in practice, stat permission errors are uncommon, and the read-phase catch correctly handles most permission issues. However, the error message could be misleading in that edge case.\n\n**AC (FR-006-3)**: File-level errors are distinguishable from YAML syntax errors in the output.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/validate-yaml.ts`:17,30,38,55\n- Evidence: File-level errors use the format `Error: File not found - {path}` or `Error: Cannot read file - {path}`, while YAML syntax errors use `Invalid YAML: {path}` followed by `Error on line N: message`. The prefixes are distinct and clearly differentiate the error types. Live tests confirmed all three error types are visually distinct.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n### FR-007: No New Production Dependencies\n\n**AC (FR-007-1)**: The `dependencies` field in `package.json` has no new entries after the feature is implemented.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/package.json`\n- Evidence: The `package.json` has no `dependencies` field at all (neither before nor after implementation). The only change to `package.json` is adding the `\"validate\"` script entry. Git diff confirms only one line added in the `scripts` block. The `devDependencies` field is unchanged (`\"@types/bun\": \"latest\"`).\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n**AC (FR-007-2)**: The YAML parsing capability comes from the runtime or from code authored within the project.\n- Status: VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/src/yaml-parser.ts` (532 lines, entirely custom)\n- Evidence: The `yaml-parser.ts` file is a fully custom TypeScript module authored within the project. It implements a line-by-line state machine parser with no external imports. The only imports in the entire feature are standard Node.js/Bun APIs (`node:fs/promises`, `node:path`, `node:os`) and the project's own `./yaml-parser` module. No YAML parsing library is used.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: None\n\n### Documentation Tasks\n\n**TD1**: Update README.md - Scripts section\n- Status: NOT VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/README.md`\n- Evidence: The README.md Scripts section lists only `bun run dev`, `bun test`, `bun run lint`, and `bun run format`. The `bun run validate <file.yaml>` entry is missing.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: README does not document the new validate command.\n\n**TD2**: Update README.md - Structure section\n- Status: NOT VERIFIED\n- Implementation: `/private/tmp/rp1-evals/f21bc400/workspace/README.md`\n- Evidence: The README.md Structure section lists only `index.ts` and `utils.ts`. The `validate-yaml.ts` and `yaml-parser.ts` entries are missing.\n- Field Notes: N/A\n- Issues: README does not reflect the new source files.\n\n## Implementation Gap Analysis\n\n### Missing Implementations\n- **TD1**: README.md Scripts section does not include `bun run validate <file.yaml>` with description.\n- **TD2**: README.md Structure section does not include `validate-yaml.ts` and `yaml-parser.ts`.\n\n### Partial Implementations\n- **AC (FR-006-2)**: Permissions error handling at the `stat()` level would produce a \"File not found\" message rather than \"Cannot read file\". The read-phase catch correctly handles permission errors, but stat-level permission errors are slightly misleading. This is a minor edge case.\n\n### Implementation Issues\n- None. All core functional requirements are correctly implemented.\n\n## Code Quality Assessment\n\n**Overall Quality: HIGH**\n\nThe implementation demonstrates strong engineering practices:\n\n1. **Separation of Concerns**: The parser logic (`yaml-parser.ts`) is cleanly separated from the CLI entry point (`validate-yaml.ts`). The parser is a pure function with no side effects, making it highly testable.\n\n2. **Type Safety**: The `YamlValidationResult` interface provides