        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-02-20\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nThe project needs a command-line capability that allows users to validate YAML files for correctness. This command will confirm whether a given YAML file is well-formed and report any syntax errors with clear, actionable messages. The validator must be implemented using only the capabilities already available in the project's existing dependency tree -- no new external packages may be introduced.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nUsers working with YAML configuration or data files currently have no built-in way to check whether those files are syntactically valid within this tool. They must rely on external tools or manual inspection, which slows down workflows and increases the risk of deploying malformed YAML.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces user friction by providing a single, integrated validation step.\n- Prevents downstream errors caused by malformed YAML reaching later stages of a pipeline.\n- Keeps the project lightweight by avoiding additional dependency bloat.\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Valid YAML files are correctly accepted | 100% of well-formed YAML input returns a success result |\n| Invalid YAML files are correctly rejected | 100% of malformed YAML input returns an error result with a meaningful message |\n| No new dependencies introduced | `package.json` dependency list remains unchanged after feature delivery |\n| Command execution completes within a reasonable time | Validation of a typical YAML file (under 10,000 lines) completes in under 2 seconds |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Relevance |\n|-----------|-------------|-----------|\n| CLI User | A developer or operator who invokes the project's commands from a terminal | Primary user who will run the YAML validation command directly |\n| Automation Consumer | A script or CI pipeline that calls the project's commands programmatically | Relies on predictable exit codes and machine-parseable output to determine pass/fail |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Project Maintainer | Keep the dependency footprint at zero new additions; maintain code simplicity |\n| End User | Get fast, clear feedback about YAML file validity |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- A new CLI command (or subcommand) that accepts one or more file paths as input and validates each as YAML.\n- Reporting of syntax errors including, at minimum, the nature of the error and the approximate location (line number) in the file.\n- A clear success message when a file is valid.\n- Appropriate exit codes: zero for success (all files valid), non-zero for failure (one or more files invalid or not found).\n- Unit and/or integration tests covering valid YAML, invalid YAML, missing files, and empty input scenarios.\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (validating YAML content against a JSON Schema, OpenAPI spec, or custom schema). This feature only checks syntactic well-formedness.\n- YAML file editing, formatting, or auto-fixing.\n- Watching files for changes and re-validating automatically.\n- Support for non-YAML formats (JSON, TOML, etc.) within this command.\n- Adding any new runtime or development dependencies to `package.json`.\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- The Bun runtime's built-in capabilities or the project's existing dependency tree provide sufficient YAML parsing support to detect syntax errors without adding new packages.\n- Users will provide file paths as command-line arguments.\n- YAML files to be validated reside on the local filesystem and are accessible to the running process.\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### FR-01: Single File Validation\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | CLI User, Automation Consumer |\n| **Requirement** | The command must accept a file path argument and determine whether the file contains syntactically valid YAML. |\n| **Rationale** | This is the core use case -- a user needs to check one YAML file for correctness. |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | 1. Given a path to a well-formed YAML file, the command outputs a success message and exits with code 0. 2. Given a path to a malformed YAML file, the command outputs an error message describing the syntax problem and exits with a non-zero code. |\n\n### FR-02: Error Reporting with Location\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | CLI User |\n| **Requirement** | When validation fails, the output must include the file name, the line number (if determinable) where the error was detected, and a human-readable description of the problem. |\n| **Rationale** | Users need enough detail to locate and fix the problem without guessing. |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | 1. Error output includes the file path that failed. 2. Error output includes a line number or position indicator when the parser provides one. 3. Error output includes a descriptive message (not just \"invalid\"). |\n\n### FR-03: Multiple File Validation\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Priority** | Should Have |\n| **User Type** | CLI User, Automation Consumer |\n| **Requirement** | The command should accept multiple file path arguments and validate each one, reporting results for all files rather than stopping at the first failure. |\n| **Rationale** | Users working with several config files benefit from validating them all in a single invocation. |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | 1. Given multiple file paths, the command validates each and reports individual pass/fail results. 2. The exit code is non-zero if any file is invalid or missing. 3. The exit code is zero only if all files are valid. |\n\n### FR-04: Missing or Unreadable File Handling\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | CLI User, Automation Consumer |\n| **Requirement** | The command must handle the case where a specified file does not exist or cannot be read, producing a clear error message rather than an unhandled exception. |\n| **Rationale** | Robustness in the face of user error (typos in file paths) is a basic quality expectation. |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | 1. Given a path to a non-existent file, the command outputs an error message stating the file was not found. 2. The exit code is non-zero. 3. No unhandled exception or stack trace is shown to the user. |\n\n### FR-05: No-Argument Usage Guidance\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Priority** | Should Have |\n| **User Type** | CLI User |\n| **Requirement** | When invoked without any file path arguments, the command should display a brief usage message explaining the expected input. |\n| **Rationale** | Helps users who discover the command but are unsure how to use it. |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | 1. Running the command with no arguments outputs a usage/help message. 2. The exit code is non-zero (no files were validated). |\n\n### FR-06: Zero New Dependencies\n\n| Attribute | Detail |\n|-----------|--------|\n| **Priority** | Must Have |\n| **User Type** | Project Maintainer |\n| **Requirement** | The implementation must not introduce any new entries in `dependencies` or `devDependencies` in `package.json`. The validator must rely solely on existing project capabilities. |\n| **Rationale** | The project values a minimal dependency footprint, and the requirement explicitly forbids adding new deps. |\n| **Acceptance Criteria** | 1. After delivery, `package.json` has no new entries in `dependencies` or `devDependencies` compared to the current state (only `@types/bun` as a devDependency). 2. No new lock file entries for YAML parsing libraries. |\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Expectation | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Response time | Validation of a single YAML file up to 10,000 lines should complete in under 2 seconds on typical developer hardware. |\n| Resource usage | The command should not consume disproportionate memory for the size of the file being validated. |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| File access | The command must only read files explicitly specified by the user. It must not traverse directories or read files beyond those provided as arguments. |\n| No code execution | The validator must not evaluate or execute any code embedded within the YAML content (e.g., YAML tags that could trigger code execution must not be honored). |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Detail |\n|-------------|--------|\n| Clear output | Success and failure messages must be immediately understandable without consulting documentation. |\n| Consistent exit codes | Exit code 0 for all-valid, non-zero for any failure, matching standard CLI conventions. |\n| Stderr vs stdout | Error messages should be directed to stderr; success confirmations may use stdout. |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\nNo specific regulatory or compliance requirements apply to this feature.\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-01: Validate a Single Configuration File\n\n**As a** CLI User\n**I want** to run a command with a YAML file path and see whether it is valid\n**So that** I can catch syntax errors before using the file in a downstream process\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios:**\n\n**Scenario A -- Valid File**\n- GIVEN a YAML file that is syntactically correct\n- WHEN I run the validate command with the path to that file\n- THEN I see a success message indicating the file is valid\n- AND the process exits with code 0\n\n**Scenario B -- Invalid File**\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error (e.g., bad indentation, unclosed quote)\n- WHEN I run the validate command with the path to that file\n- THEN I see an error message that includes the file name, approximate line number, and a description of the problem\n- AND the process exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-02: Validate Multiple Files at Once\n\n**As a** CLI User\n**I want** to pass multiple YAML file paths to the command\n**So that** I can validate an entire set of configuration files in one step\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios:**\n\n**Scenario A -- All Valid**\n- GIVEN three YAML files, all syntactically correct\n- WHEN I run the validate command with all three paths\n- THEN I see a success message for each file\n- AND the process exits with code 0\n\n**Scenario B -- Mixed Results**\n- GIVEN three YAML files where one has a syntax error\n- WHEN I run the validate command with all three paths\n- THEN I see a success message for the two valid files and an error message for the invalid one\n- AND the process exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-03: Handle Missing Files Gracefully\n\n**As a** CLI User\n**I want** to see a clear error if I accidentally specify a non-existent file\n**So that** I immediately know the path was wrong rather than seeing a cryptic stack trace\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios:**\n\n- GIVEN a file path that does not exist on disk\n- WHEN I run the validate command with that path\n- THEN I see a message like \"File not found: <path>\"\n- AND the process exits with a non-zero code\n\n### STORY-04: CI Pipeline Integration\n\n**As an** Automation Consumer\n**I want** the command to return predictable exit codes\n**So that** my CI pipeline can gate deployments on YAML validity\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios:**\n\n- GIVEN a CI step that runs the validate command\n- WHEN all specified YAML files are valid\n- THEN the step succeeds (exit code 0)\n- AND WHEN any file is invalid or missing\n- THEN the step fails (non-zero exit code)\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule |\n|---------|------|\n| BR-01 | A file is considered \"valid YAML\" if and only if it can be parsed without syntax errors according to the YAML specification. Semantic correctness (e.g., whether values match an expected schema) is not assessed. |\n| BR-02 | An empty file is considered valid YAML (it represents a null document). |\n| BR-03 | A file containing multiple YAML documents (separated by `---`) should have each document validated; the file is invalid if any document within it has a syntax error. |\n| BR-04 | No new packages may be added to `package.json` to support this feature. |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n| Type | Detail |\n|------|--------|\n| **Runtime Constraint** | Must run under the Bun runtime, consistent with the existing project setup. |\n| **Dependency Constraint** | No new npm packages may be introduced. The implementation must use built-in Bun/Node.js APIs or the existing dependency tree. |\n| **Existing Codebase** | The project currently has a CLI entry point at `src/index.ts` that processes `process.argv`. The new command should integrate consistently with this pattern. |\n| **Testing** | Tests must use `bun:test` (the existing test framework) and be runnable via `bun test`. |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\nNo interactive clarifications were conducted (AFK mode enabled). All ambiguities were resolved via inference. See the AFK Mode sections below for details.\n\n---\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | No PRD associated | No PRDs exist in `.rp1/work/prds/`. Feature proceeds without a parent PRD. |\n| KB loading | Skipped -- KB files absent | Neither `index.md`, `concept_map.md`, nor `charter.md` exist in `.rp1/context/`. Continued with best-effort analysis of the codebase directly. |\n| Command interface style | CLI subcommand via file path arguments | Inferred from existing pattern in `src/index.ts` which reads `process.argv`. No flags/options framework exists, so a simple positional-argument approach is most consistent. |\n| Multi-document YAML handling | Validate all documents in a multi-doc file | Conservative default: if a file uses `---` separators, each document should be checked rather than only the first. |\n| Empty file treatment | Treat as valid | Per YAML spec, an empty stream is a valid YAML stream containing no documents. Conservative default avoids false negatives. |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| \"simple\" -- undefined complexity boundary | Interpreted as: single-purpose command, minimal configuration surface, no interactive prompts, no advanced features like schema validation or watch mode | Requirement wording \"simple yaml validator\" combined with zero-dependency constraint |\n| \"command\" -- unclear whether standalone script or subcommand | Interpreted as a new command entry point or subcommand within the existing CLI structure, invocable via a `bun run` script or direct execution | Existing project structure: `src/index.ts` serves as CLI entry, `package.json` defines `scripts` |\n| \"without adding new deps\" -- scope of restriction | Interpreted as: no new entries in either `dependencies` or `devDependencies` in `package.json` | Literal reading of the requirement; project currently has only `@types/bun` as a dev dependency |\n| \"yaml validator\" -- syntax-only or schema validation | Interpreted as syntax/parse validation only (well-formedness check), not schema or semantic validation | \"Simple\" qualifier and zero-dependency constraint make schema validation out of scope |\n| Actor not specified | Inferred two actor types: CLI User (interactive) and Automation Consumer (CI/scripts) | Standard CLI tool usage patterns; no user roles defined in project context |\n| Exit code behavior | Zero for all-valid, non-zero for any failure | Standard Unix CLI conventions; required for CI integration |\n"
