        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator-command\n**Created**: 2026-02-20\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | YAML parsing approach | Custom hand-written syntax validator in TypeScript | Zero-dependency constraint (FR-06) prohibits adding npm packages like `js-yaml` or `yaml`. No built-in YAML parser confirmed in the Bun runtime or existing dependency tree. A custom validator is the only viable path under current constraints. | (a) Use `js-yaml` npm package -- rejected, violates FR-06. (b) Use a hypothetical Bun built-in YAML parser -- not confirmed to exist, flagged as hypothesis HYP-001. (c) Shell out to an external tool (e.g., Python's PyYAML) -- rejected, adds external runtime dependency and breaks portability. |\n| D2 | Command integration style | Separate entry point script (`src/validate-yaml.ts`) with new `package.json` script | The existing `src/index.ts` is a simple single-purpose entry point (greeting). Adding subcommand dispatch would change its behavior and add unwarranted complexity. A separate entry point is cleaner, follows the single-responsibility principle, and avoids modifying existing functionality. | (a) Subcommand dispatch in `src/index.ts` (e.g., `bun run dev validate ...`) -- rejected, existing `index.ts` has no command routing and changing it is out of scope. (b) Standalone script outside `src/` -- rejected, inconsistent with existing structure. |\n| D3 | File I/O API | Bun.file() for existence checks and content reading | Native Bun API, idiomatic for a Bun project, provides async file operations with clean API (`exists()`, `text()`). No additional imports needed. | (a) Node.js `fs` module -- would work but less idiomatic for a Bun-first project. (b) `Bun.read()` -- `Bun.file()` is the standard approach. |\n| D4 | Output routing | Success messages to stdout, error messages to stderr | Explicitly required by NFR 6.3. Follows standard Unix CLI conventions. Enables automation consumers to separate success output from error output via standard stream redirection. | (a) All output to stdout -- rejected, violates requirements and Unix conventions. (b) Structured JSON output -- not required; plain text is simpler and meets stated requirements. |\n| D5 | Multi-document YAML handling | Split on `---` boundaries, validate each document independently with line offset tracking | Required by BR-03. Splitting first allows independent validation of each document while maintaining accurate line numbers by tracking the offset of each document's start within the original file. | (a) Validate as single blob -- rejected, misses per-document errors and violates BR-03. (b) Streaming parser -- unnecessarily complex for a syntax validator. |\n| D6 | File structure | Flat in `src/` directory (`yaml-parser.ts`, `validate-yaml.ts`) | Consistent with existing structure where all source files are flat in `src/`. No subdirectories exist in the current codebase. | (a) `src/commands/` subdirectory -- rejected, no existing subdirectory pattern to follow. (b) Single file for everything -- rejected, separation of parser logic from CLI logic enables independent testing. |\n| D7 | Error message format | `Error: {path}:{line}:{col} - {message}` | Follows common compiler/linter output conventions (GCC, ESLint, TypeScript). Enables editor integration for jump-to-line. Human-readable while also machine-parseable. | (a) JSON error objects -- overly complex for a CLI tool with no stated JSON output requirement. (b) Verbose multi-line format -- harder to parse programmatically. |\n| D8 | Empty file handling | Treat as valid YAML | Explicitly required by BR-02. Per the YAML specification, an empty stream is a valid YAML stream containing zero documents. | (a) Treat as error -- rejected, violates spec and BR-02. |\n| D9 | Exit code scheme | 0 = all valid, 1 = any failure | Standard Unix convention. Explicitly required by FR-01, FR-03, FR-04, FR-05, and NFR 6.3. Simple binary success/failure is sufficient; no need for multiple non-zero codes to distinguish error types. | (a) Distinct exit codes per error type (2 = parse error, 3 = file not found, etc.) -- rejected, not required and adds complexity. |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Technology Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Source | Rationale |\n|----------|--------|--------|-----------|\n| YAML parsing mechanism | Custom TypeScript implementation | Codebase analysis (zero deps in package.json) | No YAML parser available in existing dependency tree; FR-06 prohibits adding new packages |\n| Entry point pattern | Separate script file | Codebase pattern (src/index.ts is single-purpose) | Follows existing pattern of simple single-purpose entry points |\n| File I/O API | Bun.file() | Codebase context (Bun runtime) | Native Bun API, idiomatic choice for Bun projects |\n| Test framework | bun:test | Codebase pattern (src/utils.test.ts) | Only test framework in the project; required by dependency constraint |\n| Test file placement | Co-located in src/ | Codebase pattern (src/utils.test.ts) | Matches existing co-located test file pattern |\n| Script naming | `validate-yaml` | Conservative default | Descriptive, kebab-case consistent with existing script names (`dev`, `test`, `lint`, `format`) |\n| Multi-document handling | Validate all documents | Requirements (BR-03) + conservative default | Requirement explicitly states multi-doc validation; conservative approach validates all rather than just first |\n| Output format | Plain text (not JSON) | Conservative default | Requirements mention \"clear, actionable messages\" and \"human-readable\"; no JSON output requirement stated |\n"
